<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Hardened Runtime, not App Sandbox — this is the `dmg` target (direct
	distribution + notarization), not `mas` (Mac App Store), so the App
	Sandbox entitlement set (com.apple.security.app-sandbox and its
	per-resource exceptions) does not apply here and must not be added by
	analogy with an App Sandbox guide.

	Three keys, and all three are Hardened Runtime keys:

	- allow-jit and allow-unsigned-executable-memory are what V8/Node need
	  to keep running under Hardened Runtime.

	- device.audio-input is Hardened Runtime's own "Resource Access" gate
	  for the microphone. Apple's Hardened Runtime page lists it there,
	  beside camera, location and Apple events — it is NOT only an App
	  Sandbox key, which is what an earlier revision of this comment (and
	  docs/MACOS-SIGNING.md, and CLAUDE.md) claimed. A hardened process
	  without it is refused by TCC before any prompt: the singer never sees
	  a permission request, `systemPreferences.askForMediaAccess` resolves
	  false while the access status stays "not-determined", and CoreAudio
	  input delivers silence. That was the field report against the signed
	  v0.19.1–v0.20.1 builds — "no permission request, and no mic" — while
	  every ad-hoc dev build on the dev Mac worked, because an ad-hoc
	  signature carries no `runtime` flag and so is never held to this key.
	  NSMicrophoneUsageDescription (electron-builder.yml, mac.extendInfo) is
	  still required too — it is the text of the prompt — but it does not
	  stand in for this entitlement.

	The same key is in entitlements.mac.inherit.plist, on purpose: see the
	note there for which processes actually open the device.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
